<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- OstrichChallenge: HONEY IN THE DRINKS MACHINES

     Honey cures infection, and in a building with no traders and no doctor it is the only cure
     the player can reliably plan around. Putting it behind Dukes rather than behind luck makes
     an infection a setback you can work your way out of instead of a run that quietly ends.

     WHERE IT GOES, AND WHY NOT THE OBVIOUS PLACE.
     The drinks machine is trader id 4 (is_vending="true", reset_interval="1"), and it stocks
     from two groups:

         <trader_items>
             <item group="drinkVending" count="4,8"/>
             <item group="drinkSpecialVending" count="4,8"/>
         </trader_items>

     drinkVending is a wrapper holding a single entry, <item group="groupDrinks"/>. Appending
     honey THERE would have been tidier and better scoped, and I did not, deliberately: with
     count="4,8" drawn from a group that would then hold two entries, the outcome depends on
     whether the picker draws with replacement or simply takes everything up to the count. If it
     is the latter, honey appears in every machine on every restock, guaranteed. I cannot read
     that behaviour out of the XML and would be guessing, so this goes one level down instead.

     Inside groupDrinks honey is one entry among eight, so it is bounded however the picker
     works. That is the whole reason for the choice.

     THE COST OF THAT CHOICE, stated plainly: groupDrinks is also referenced by groupFoodAll and
     traderGeneral, so honey is now very slightly more likely at a general trader too. Occupancy
     has no traders and the player cannot leave the hotel, so in this mod that is academic. It
     would matter if this file were reused elsewhere.

     THE RATE. The seven vanilla drinks carry no prob, which is a weight of 1.0 each. Honey at
     0.5 is therefore about 0.5/7.5, near enough 7% of each pick. At 4 to 8 picks per restock
     that is roughly one honey every other restock per working machine.

     PRICE IS THE REAL GATE. foodHoney is EconomicValue 80 and trader 4 carries
     override_buy_markup="3", so a jar costs about 240 Dukes. The player has to have cracked a
     few safes before an infection is survivable, which is the intended shape: reachable, not free.

     TO DIAL IT: raise prob towards 1.0 to match the other drinks (12.5% of picks), or drop it to
     0.25 for roughly one honey every fourth restock.

     Every xpath here was resolved against the real vanilla traders.xml before shipping.
-->
<configs>

    <append xpath="/traders/trader_item_groups/trader_item_group[@name='groupDrinks']">
        <item name="foodHoney" count="1,2" prob="0.5"/>
    </append>

    <!-- ================================================================================
         CHOC 'N' AWE IN THE SNACK MACHINES (v1.13.5)

         Fril: "Add choc n awe to the vending machines but dont make it cheap. It needs to be
         more expensive than the chili as there needs to be a premium on it not smwlling when
         you eat it."

         WHERE, AND IT IS THE SAME REASONING AS THE HONEY ABOVE. The food vending machine is
         trader id 10, and its chain is two links:

             trader_info id=10  ->  <item group="foodVending" count="4,8"/>
             foodVending        ->  <item group="cannedfood"/>
             cannedfood         ->  15 tinned items, no prob on any of them

         Appending to foodVending would have been the tidier place and is the trap the honey
         note describes: with count="4,8" drawn from a group that would then hold two entries,
         the bar appears in every machine on every restock if the picker takes everything up to
         the count rather than drawing with replacement. That behaviour still cannot be read out
         of the XML, so this goes one level down again.

         Inside cannedfood the bar is one entry among sixteen, so it is bounded however the
         picker works. NO prob, matching the fifteen tinned items, which all carry an implicit
         weight of 1.0. So roughly 1 in 16 of each pick, 4 to 8 picks a restock: it turns up
         often enough to be a real option and not so often that it is the default lunch.

         PRICE IS THE GATE, NOT RARITY, exactly as it is for the honey. 240 Dukes against the
         chili's 180, which is level with the honey in the drinks machine. The full arithmetic
         and the dial are in items.xml beside EconomicValue.

         THE COST OF GOING ONE LEVEL DOWN, stated plainly the way the honey note does it:
         cannedfood is referenced in four places in vanilla traders.xml, not just by the vending
         chain, so the bar becomes very slightly likelier at a general trader too. The hotel has
         no traders and the player cannot leave, so in this mod that is academic. It would matter
         if this file were reused elsewhere.

         NOT MEASURED: no machine has been bought from since this went in.
         ================================================================================ -->
    <append xpath="/traders/trader_item_groups/trader_item_group[@name='cannedfood']">
        <item name="occChocNAwe" count="1,2"/>
    </append>

    <!-- ================================================================================
         FRILIOTH'S OWN TRADER ENTRY, WITH NOTHING IN IT (v0.80.0)

         He is the end of the run, not a shop. The survivor drives across the city, finds him,
         and the game ends. A stock list would be strange at that point, so he has none.

         WHY HE NEEDS AN ENTRY AT ALL RATHER THAN NO trader_id.
         Two reasons, both read out of the assembly rather than assumed:

           1. TraderData.get_TraderInfo is

                  if (TraderID == -1) return null;
                  return traderInfoList[TraderID];

              so a trader whose id has no entry behind it gets a NULL TraderInfo, and everything
              that then asks it a question throws. The npc.xml entry and this one are a pair and
              must always ship together.

           2. EntityTrader.PostInit only sets IsGodMode inside "if (NPCInfo.TraderID > 0)". He is
              the ending, so he must not be killable by a stray shot or a wandering horde. Keeping
              a real, positive id is what keeps that protection.

         WHY ID 11.
         traderInfoList is a fixed ARRAY, indexed directly by the id, allocated as

              ldc.i4 0x00000100 ; newarr TraderInfo

         so it holds 256 slots and ids 0 to 255 are all legal. Vanilla fills 1 to 10 (1 Joel,
         2 Jen, 3 player-owned, 4 vending, 5 rentable, 6 Bob, 7 Hugh, 8 Rekt, 9 traderTest,
         10 vending), so 11 is the first free one. An earlier note in npc.xml said 10 was free.
         That was wrong: it came from a list I had in my head rather than from this file.

         NO OPENING HOURS HERE ON PURPOSE. Trader hours are a sandbox setting in 3.2 and Fril is
         setting his to 24 hours, so there is nothing to declare and nothing that can shut the
         ending behind a closed door at ten past ten at night.

         reset_interval="-1" is vanilla's own "never restocks", used by ids 3 and 5. There is
         nothing to restock, and it stops the game scheduling resets for an empty list.

         allow_sell="false" is included because "no trades" cuts both ways: with it, the survivor
         cannot sell him things either. Say the word and it comes out.

         An empty self-closing trader_info is legal and vanilla does it: ids 3 and 5 both carry no
         trader_items at all.
         ================================================================================ -->

    <append xpath="/traders">
        <trader_info id="11" reset_interval="-1" allow_sell="false"/>
    </append>

</configs>
